00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:01.850 align:start position:0%
 
the government of the Indian Capital  Delhi is installing CCTV cameras in  School classrooms it says it is to keep  students safe that reasoning born out of  a series of sexual assaults and rapes  against students in schools some  perpetrated by senior school students  themselves some by School Employees and  others by Outsiders  more than a thousand schools run by the  Delhi government are to be covered by  the scheme but its dividing opinion  across the board as DW correspondent  Adil Bhatt reports  foreign  School in Delhi became one of the first  schools to install CCTV cameras in its  classrooms in public areas  every day hundreds of students here are  constantly under surveillance with each  movement of their day being filmed  from the comfort of his office principal  Rakesh dhaya can monitor everything  happening at his school  he says that camera footage allows him  to quickly tackle problems like bullying  and petty crime  something is there okay a warning system  you can say there's some students if  they have some kind of  prank or something to there also this  having the idea okay you are being  watched everything is being recorded So  that adds to that discipline quotient  you don't feel uncomfortable at being  under the Gaze of the almost 200 cameras  in the building  I think  no student would want to be watched by  cameras in the classroom and I'm sure if  you were a student you wouldn't want  that either  so far cameras have been installed in  over 500 schools across the state  concerned with the growing number of  cameras  the Delhi parents Association has taken  the state government to code  today parents Association had aparjita  Gotham and her team are meeting their  lawyer to discuss the case  they are preparing for a hearing in the  coming week  says that live streaming in the schools  jeopardizes the Safety and Security of  young girls and could lead to digital  warism  from the perspective of parents I think  nobody has the right to see if my child  or daughter is studying or not studying  in the classroom  not even parents have that right I see  this move by the Delhi government as  encouraging a culture of live stalking  Freedom Foundation an organization that  defends privacy rights and online  freedom is also critical of the mass  surveillance  Wisconsin surveillance of children also  has a negative impact on their Social  Development so what is happening  essentially is that surveillance of  children is being increased in the name  of making them more secure they're not  getting more secure instead they are now  being harmed as increased security at  the cost of privacy is hotly disputed  around the world  India continues to rise up the list of  countries who watch their citizens more  closely  the debate in Delhi is where the School  classrooms should be the focus of such  surveillance many parents and students  think not  and joining me now for more context is  Dr Sangeetha mahapatra from Giga that's  the German Institute for Global and area  studies and part of Dr mahapatra's  research looks at the impact of digital  surveillance on civil liberties Dr  mahapatra welcome what does the research  say does camera surveillance in  classrooms keep students safe  uh thank you for having me here uh I it  is very different because I have been  studying schools in Delhi in Calcutta in  Bangalore  so while CCTV cameras are becoming  standard practice there is no  standardization of best practices  however in the schools that I have been  to one these are mostly city-based  schools there have been uh The Bullying  come down to an extent in the schools  that I have gone to and interacted with  parents with faculty members with  students themselves  so when it comes to uh bullying  incidents yes to an extent CCTV cameras  have reduced these incidents of stalking  of harassment of bullying I I had a lot  of my uh assumptions also challenged  when I did field work in these because I  assume that students would be uh could  be  would try to change their behavior when  it comes to the cameras inside  classrooms however after point of time I  saw that they got used to it and they  were not that aware of the cameras being  there which was quite revelatory of the  kind of internalization of the  surveillance mechanisms that is  happening so just based on what you're  saying the Delhi government would appear  to have the correct argument that they  are keeping children safe by installing  CCTV cameras in Delhi government schools  is that correct  I would like to give it a kind of a  caveat here because  because you have a reasonable  expectation of privacy that is that you  cannot come and put cameras inside  restrooms or in certain areas that they  consider to be really private  and here this has to align with the  purpose for which the data is being  connected as well as the processes in  place to prevent the misuse of data and  this varies from school to school  I go back to my point about  standardization of practices while some  schools have put in place robust  mechanisms where data cannot be misused  some schools haven't there hasn't been  that sensitization when it comes to how  do you handle that data for how long do  you handle that data who has access to  it for how long is it stored all these  questions are very important when it  comes to especially children when their  data has to be handled with extra care  and there has to be extra due diligence  here right so yes parents are very happy  with the decision of jail the government  that majorities of the parents that I  spoke to  for them it was a confidence building  measure with schools during orientation  when they had this parent Council  meetings  uh just on going back to the point of  purpose and a process and the Digital  Data Protections in India would you say  that the Way digital data is handled and  the protections around that differ to  the way they are differ in India to the  way they are handled potentially in the  west particularly the sensitization  around that  by arrest also there is difference like  in Germany it is pretty good the  standard when it was gdpr are there and  there is also the EU rules when it comes  to the fundamental rights of Digital  Data protection but in India it is lacks  the current digital personal data  protection act uh bill which is this is  third variant till now it hasn't been  passed and it gives God Blanche to the  government there are a lot of wide  exemptions and there are real privacy  concerns that's the first problem we  don't have uh data protection bill at in  place second concern is we don't have a  statutory body of data protection where  people can raise consult secret wrestle  so that isn't there so it's pretty lacks  the data protection act but this is at  the macro level what I would like to  stress here is that when it comes to  schools that's at the micro level and  where they deal with parents and parents  are pretty vocal about their children's  protection at least now and mind you let  me put in one uh information here that  CCTV cameras have become a standard  practice across India from 2017 onwards  for the past six years I have been  seeing this in Bangalore in Telangana in  Calcutta in Mumbai recently in Mumbai  all the 65 000 Civic and private schools  have brought in CCTV cameras  so these are also there but the  concomitant data protection measures  those have not been in place so again I  stress that some schools do good work  they have these control rooms where very  few people have access to the data feed  while some schools just put it in a  reception area where anyone can see what  is happening in the CCTV camera so it's  not standardized let's look at the  search interruptive let's just look at  the non-digital side of this argument  because the government's claim continues  to be looked we want to keep students  safe therefore we need to have CCTV  cameras and based on some of the  experiences that you have had it seems  to be working at least in some schools  let's look at the other side of it is  this also a case of the government  washing its hands of its own  responsibility to ensure that School  Employees protect children  this is actually a very good question  because uh there this also differs from  state to state like in Delhi government  one of the flagship areas is education  and health and here there is this  consultation constantly happening  between the government officials and  schools about especially the schools  that are getting government support  so here the government cannot wash off  its hands of security of children when  it comes to this place because these are  the schools are accountable to the  government and the government is  ultimately accountable to the voters the  parents so in these areas they don't  private schools here anyway have some  amount of autonomy when it comes to uh  safety of children but here too one sees  X for example in Kolkata for example we  see that the municipal Corporation is uh  talking to these schools and trying to  install CCTV cameras outside the  premises to prevent incidences of  violence of crime especially they  started with schools which are girls  only girls schools or co-educational  institutions  so we see mixed picture from state to  state so here so I don't think we can  have a very simple answer here too  certainly a very different school time  to the time that I was in school which  was many years ago thank you so much for  joining us today Dr Sangeetha mahapatra  for that context thank you so much  my pleasure thank you

